Both graphs show the same library loans. Library A recorded $84$ loans and Library B recorded $88$ loans. Which graph gives a misleading impression? Explain why, and explain what makes the other version fair.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw[black,->] (0,0)--(0,5.5);
\draw[black,->] (0,0)--(4,0);
\foreach \p/\v in {0/80,1/82,2/84,3/86,4/88,5/90}{\draw[black] (-0.1,\p)--(0,\p);\path (-0.15,\p) node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};}
\draw[black] (0.6,0) rectangle (1.4,2);
\draw[black] (2.2,0) rectangle (3,4);
\path (1,0) node[below] {A} (2.6,0) node[below] {B};
\path (1.8,6) node {Graph P} (-1.8,2.5) node[rotate=90] {Loans} (1.8,-1.3) node {Library};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw[black,->] (0,0)--(0,5.5);
\draw[black,->] (0,0)--(4,0);
\foreach \p/\v in {0/0,1/20,2/40,3/60,4/80,5/100}{\draw[black] (-0.1,\p)--(0,\p);\path (-0.15,\p) node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};}
\draw[black] (0.6,0) rectangle (1.4,4.2);
\draw[black] (2.2,0) rectangle (3,4.4);
\path (1,0) node[below] {A} (2.6,0) node[below] {B};
\path (1.8,6) node {Graph Q} (-1.8,2.5) node[rotate=90] {Loans} (1.8,-1.3) node {Library};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The graph shows weekly ticket sales. Venue A sold $56$ tickets and Venue B sold $60$. Identify two features that exaggerate the difference.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=12mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw[black,->] (0,0)--(0,5.6);
\draw[black,->] (0,0)--(5,0);
\foreach \p/\v in {0/40,1/45,2/50,3/55,4/60,5/65}{\draw[black] (-0.1,\p)--(0,\p);\path (-0.15,\p) node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};}
\draw[black] (0.7,0) rectangle (1.3,3.2);
\draw[black] (2.5,0) rectangle (4.3,4);
\path (1,0) node[below] {A} (3.4,0) node[below] {B};
\path (2.5,6) node {Weekly ticket sales} (-0.9,2.7) node[rotate=90] {Tickets sold} (2.5,-0.8) node {Venue};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Each square represents a year's number of visitors. The number rose from $150$ to $300$, and the second square has twice the width and twice the height of the first. Explain why the pictograph exaggerates the increase.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=12mm,y=12mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw[black] (0,0) rectangle (1,1);
\draw[black] (3,0) rectangle (5,2);
\path (0.5,0) node[below] {2023} (4,0) node[below] {2024};
\path (0.5,1) node[above] {$150$} (4,2) node[above] {$300$};
\path (2.5,3) node {Annual visitors};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

This graph shows club membership in three years. Explain why it creates the impression that membership increased at a steady rate.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=12mm,y=10mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{seriesblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,->] (0,0)--(0,4);
\draw[black,->] (0,0)--(5.6,0);
\foreach \p/\v in {0/0,1/20,2/40,3/60}{\draw[black] (-0.1,\p)--(0,\p);\path (-0.15,\p) node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};}
\foreach \x/\v in {1/2018,3/2019,5/2023}{\draw[black] (\x,0)--(\x,-0.1);\path (\x,-0.15) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};}
\draw[seriesblue,thick] (1,1)--(3,2)--(5,3);
\fill[seriesblue] (1,1) circle (1.5pt);
\fill[seriesblue] (3,2) circle (1.5pt);
\fill[seriesblue] (5,3) circle (1.5pt);
\path (2.8,4.5) node {Club membership} (-0.9,2) node[rotate=90] {Members} (2.8,-0.8) node {Year};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A survey let each student select one favourite activity from Sport, Music and Art. The graph claims to show all students' choices as shares of the whole survey. Explain why the reported shares cannot be correct.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=12mm,y=9mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw[black,->] (0,0)--(0,4.5);
\draw[black,->] (0,0)--(6,0);
\foreach \p/\v in {0/0,1/20,2/40,3/60,4/80}{\draw[black] (-0.1,\p)--(0,\p);\path (-0.15,\p) node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v\%$};}
\draw[black] (0.6,0) rectangle (1.6,2.5);
\draw[black] (2.4,0) rectangle (3.4,2.25);
\draw[black] (4.2,0) rectangle (5.2,1.75);
\path (1.1,0) node[below] {Sport} (2.9,0) node[below] {Music} (4.7,0) node[below] {Art};
\path (1.1,2.5) node[above] {$50\%$} (2.9,2.25) node[above] {$45\%$} (4.7,1.75) node[above] {$35\%$};
\path (3,5) node {Favourite activities} (-2,2.2) node[rotate=90] {Share of students} (3,-0.9) node {Activity};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The graph shows monthly sales. Can you determine how many more items were sold in March than in January? Explain what information is missing.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=12mm,y=10mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw[black,->] (0,0)--(0,4);
\draw[black,->] (0,0)--(5.5,0);
\draw[black] (0.6,0) rectangle (1.4,1.5);
\draw[black] (2.2,0) rectangle (3,2);
\draw[black] (3.8,0) rectangle (4.6,3);
\path (1,0) node[below] {Jan} (2.6,0) node[below] {Feb} (4.2,0) node[below] {Mar};
\path (2.7,4.5) node {Monthly sales} (-0.6,2) node[rotate=90] {Items sold} (2.7,-0.8) node {Month};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
